                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f9bbac93-d966-323e-91ce-311407dabce9",
                        "entry_id": "41fba455-ba79-30be-acb2-2efcb6d6d067",
                        "glosses": [
                            "то же, что скумбрия"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6dd70fb4-a5ce-472b-aaed-f44484145b93",
                                "sense_id": "f9bbac93-d966-323e-91ce-311407dabce9",
                                "text": "Окрестные и дальние рыбаки свозили в город рыбу: весною ― мелкую камсу, миллионами наполнявшую доверху их баркасы, летом ― уродливую камбалу, осенью ― макрель, жирную кефаль и устрицы, а зимой ― десяти — и двадцатипудовую белугу, выловленную часто с большой опасностью для жизни за много верст от берега."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "18ecc28d-21ab-4060-9512-df0f41341173",
                        "source_entry_id": "41fba455-ba79-30be-acb2-2efcb6d6d067",
                        "source_sense_id": "f9bbac93-d966-323e-91ce-311407dabce9",
                        "target_lang_word": "mackerel",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "mackerel",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "eb5ad564-db0d-3ad1-ac1b-fb2e86b60245",
                                        "entry_id": "e8afadff-1684-3341-b6fa-ce850e274ab3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Certain smaller edible fish, principally true mackerel and Spanish mackerel in family Scombridae, often speckled,"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c2645e5d-280a-3043-b8a8-4684d1edee9e",
                                        "entry_id": "e8afadff-1684-3341-b6fa-ce850e274ab3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Certain smaller edible fish, principally true mackerel and Spanish mackerel in family Scombridae, often speckled,",
                                            "typically Scomber scombrus in the British isles."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "89fea792-0fb1-49a5-8a76-75097fcb1857",
                                                "sense_id": "c2645e5d-280a-3043-b8a8-4684d1edee9e",
                                                "text": "[…] you may buy land now as cheap as stinking mackerel."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b2a9138e-290d-4300-b96f-d9704b0d890e",
                                                "sense_id": "c2645e5d-280a-3043-b8a8-4684d1edee9e",
                                                "text": "I am living fast, to see the Time, when a Book that misses its Tide, shall be neglected, as the Moon by Day, or like Mackarel a Week after the Season."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e034cd32-5fc8-4985-8a12-a0f02f66c314",
                                                "sense_id": "c2645e5d-280a-3043-b8a8-4684d1edee9e",
                                                "text": "Philander went into the next room[…]and came back with a salt mackerel that dripped brine like a rainstorm. Then he put the coffee pot on the stove and rummaged out a loaf of dry bread and some hardtack."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e0c7ca6b-a05a-44e2-9770-1d55cd2f2d08",
                                                "sense_id": "c2645e5d-280a-3043-b8a8-4684d1edee9e",
                                                "text": "He sometimes pinches the maids till their arms are as many colours as a mackerel’s back."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "04a99b89-f9a3-429b-96ce-aa27582c30b2",
                                                "sense_id": "c2645e5d-280a-3043-b8a8-4684d1edee9e",
                                                "text": "1982, Audre Lorde, Zami: A New Spelling of My Name, Chapter 5, in Zami; Sister Outsider; Undersong, New York: Quality Paperback Book Club, 1993, p. 47,\n“ […] if you ever so much as breathe a word about my stories, Sandman’s comin’ after you the very same minute to pluck out you eyes like a mackerel for soup.”"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "31219d0a-29e3-30ff-8cc9-f338653036fc",
                                        "entry_id": "e8afadff-1684-3341-b6fa-ce850e274ab3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A true mackerel, any fish of tribe Scombrini (Scomber spp., Rastrelliger spp.)"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c5d78053-e7af-3af6-a5e8-63690eb833a2",
                                        "entry_id": "e8afadff-1684-3341-b6fa-ce850e274ab3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Certain other similar small fish in families Carangidae, Gempylidae, and Hexagrammidae."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2d8c78be-2c45-38e8-94a4-aa912cae7093",
                                        "entry_id": "e8afadff-1684-3341-b6fa-ce850e274ab3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A regular pattern, similar to fish scales, of undulating small clouds with sky visible between them."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "attributive",
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "92746a41-5314-4eb5-a48e-bb92ec2243ae",
                                                "sense_id": "2d8c78be-2c45-38e8-94a4-aa912cae7093",
                                                "text": "a mackerel sky"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c2cf498e-9f12-498a-8b93-656a98965589",
                                                "sense_id": "2d8c78be-2c45-38e8-94a4-aa912cae7093",
                                                "text": "MACKEREL CLOUDS.- Mackerel scales and mares' tails / Make lofty ships carry low sails."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "330319a9-30b5-4749-870a-c322a7398e7f",
                                                "sense_id": "2d8c78be-2c45-38e8-94a4-aa912cae7093",
                                                "text": "Mackerel clouds in sky, Expect more wet than dry. A mackerel sky, Not twenty-four hours dry."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c219b3e5-11d9-43f4-8761-36e77384e2ab",
                                                "sense_id": "2d8c78be-2c45-38e8-94a4-aa912cae7093",
                                                "text": "Mackerel in the sky, three days dry. […] Mackerel sky, mackerel sky - never long wet, never long dry."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "mackerel",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "78fcaae7-050e-3c8d-aebb-d81cf7c4814e",
                                        "entry_id": "e40b64c4-bc65-35ce-807b-5fdee2ebe347",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A pimp; also, a bawd."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0ba579b2-e673-4341-8ac8-431bb10923dd",
                                                "sense_id": "78fcaae7-050e-3c8d-aebb-d81cf7c4814e",
                                                "text": "You can't 'work' in a legal brothel without mackerel."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1106e60d-9268-4d17-864a-c431c85fd704",
                                                "sense_id": "78fcaae7-050e-3c8d-aebb-d81cf7c4814e",
                                                "text": "Perhaps, but my sources think the mackerel knew of this girl but she didn't know of him."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "835f8ed9-15ae-45fa-9dc6-09c8f31f4dfd",
                                                "sense_id": "78fcaae7-050e-3c8d-aebb-d81cf7c4814e",
                                                "text": "1483, William Caxton, Magnus Cato, quoted in James Orchard Halliwell-Phillipps, A Dictionary of Archaic and Provincial Words, Obsolete Phrases, Proverbs and Ancient Customs, from the Fourteenth Century, vol. 2, publ. by John Russell Smith (1847), page 536.\n[…] nyghe his hows dwellyd a maquerel or bawde […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "16bd3113-c1c5-4ad9-9975-b34edb802883",
                                                "sense_id": "78fcaae7-050e-3c8d-aebb-d81cf7c4814e",
                                                "text": "NETTING MACKEREL: THE PIMP DETAIL"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "27a0db78-e3df-435f-8869-586fdab028f5",
                                                "sense_id": "78fcaae7-050e-3c8d-aebb-d81cf7c4814e",
                                                "text": "Hundreds of ‘night birds’ and their ‘mackerels’ and other vice-pushers were sent packing."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e178eb40-28cf-45ea-9a65-4c23fac4e25e",
                                                "sense_id": "78fcaae7-050e-3c8d-aebb-d81cf7c4814e",
                                                "text": "2006, Paul Crowley, Message-ID: in humanities.lit.authors.shakespeare https://web.archive.org/web/20201001221812/https://groups.google.com/forum/message/raw?msg=humanities.lit.authors.shakespeare/VarPp2-HSO0/QuMJdNOwfisJ\nA procurer or a pimp is a broker (or broker-between), a mackerel, or a pandar; the last is not necessarily-and, indeed, not usually-a professional."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
